at that specifies the format of a  text file used in bioinformatics course  during gene sequence variation  now that we have learned about the  difference between mutations and  variation and how to identify these  variation in the next part now we will  learn about the clinical relevance of  mutations  upon receiving the dna sequencing  results for a sick patient the first  question that is usually asked is what  genes are affected there is not a simple  answer to this question many of the dna  variants are still being discovered  annotated and rigorously analyzed by  diverse team of scientists  for example in cancer the dna variation  can help determine genetic risk  associated with oncogenes but most often  oncologists look for somatic mutations  in tumor suppressor genes oncogenes or  dna repair genes since cancer most often  occurs with age many researchers see the  accumulation of somatic mutations in key  genomic regions such as a combination of  driver and passenger mutations that can  help diagnose cancer early on evaluate  risk of cancer severity and select  appropriate treatment to slow down  inflarable metastatic cancer  next we learned about clinvar which is a  freely accessible public archive of  reports of the relationships among human  variations and phenotypes with  supporting evidence another great  repository to annotate variants for  cancer is cosmic which stands for the  catalogue of somatic mutations in cancer  here one can estimate the clinical  relevance of the variance by comparing  it with list of mutations compiled in  cosmic database  further we also briefly discussed about  loading vsaf file in a data analysis  environment like r for visualization  manipulation and analysis to be  performed on the entire data set at once  to continue learning we invite you to  explore the associated resources for  session two which can prices of course  work case studies and other examples  from literature  the contents of session two correspond  with practical lessons that you can  complete independently available on the  omixlogic portal for session 2 we have  covered concepts based on lesson from  course 3 genomics this course serves as  an introduction to the bioinformatics  subdiscipline of genomics where students  will be familiarized with the biology of  genetics and genetic variation while  considering practical applications  within the full course specific lessons  that are of relevance to session two  include lesson three introduction to  genomics that will give you an overview  of genetics and genetic variation lesson  5 genomic variation in ngs data  practical where you will learn to create  varying colon pipeline to identify  variants between cancerous and  non-cancerous you know  another lesson is lesson 9 advanced  genomics overview that will cover  advanced concepts in genomics including  single nucleotide variants various  terminologies associated with mutations  and file formats that stored variance  data another lesson is lesson 10  analyzing analysis of ngs genomic data  here you will analyze genomic data and  interpret biological insights using  jbrowse the full course contains a lot  more information that you can choose to  explore independently at your own pace  so do not worry about completing all of  them immediately  another great resource that you will  have access to is project 14 breast  cancer oncogene variant calling here you  will find answers to questions such as  what are these harmful mutations what is  their nature to what extent are these  mutations responsible for causing  diseases or disorders how can these  prominent mutations be helpful to  improve prognostication  in the upcoming session three on  phylogenetic analysis and multiple  sequence alignment you will build upon  the genomic data analysis skills that  you learned in session two so if you  have not gone through session two yet  make sure to complete the associated  coursework and example projects listed  before proc